    },
    {
      "@id": "skills/conduct/conduct-core/agent-memory.md#provenance-1",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "history",
      "cat-harness:section": "Every entry carries exactly one of three labels",
      "prov:value": "**A `baseline` with no measurement is refused by the schema**, and that rule bit\nimmediately and correctly the first time it was structural rather than prose:\nall three entries labelled BASELINE stored no number — they were *tables of\ncommands to run*. **A table of how to measure is a stable fact, not a\nmeasurement**, and they were relabelled. A watcher whose subject is a live\nsignal should hold no such numbers at all, because every one of them goes stale\nby design."
    },
    {
      "@id": "skills/conduct/conduct-core/agent-memory.md#provenance-2",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "history",
      "cat-harness:section": "Entries are authored as nodes; the file is generated",
      "prov:value": "**Edit the node; CI assembles.** The assembled file under `.claude/` is a\nprojection, and since 2026-10-09 CI writes and commits it, never an agent\n([`harness-projections`](harness-projections.md)). Run the assembler locally in\nits check form to see what it would change.",
      "cat-harness:refs": [
        "2026-10-09"
      ],
      "dcterms:date": [
        "2026-10-09"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "skills/conduct/conduct-core/agent-memory.md#provenance-3",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "history",
      "cat-harness:section": "Entries are authored as nodes; the file is generated",
      "prov:value": "**One entry can reach several agents, and that is the point.** Before this, a\nfact two agents needed had to be written into two files — measured here, three\nsubject areas were."
    },
    {
      "@id": "skills/conduct/conduct-core/agent-memory.md#provenance-4",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "bean",
      "cat-harness:section": "Vendor target mapping — one memory source, generated per target",
      "prov:value": "Gemini CLI, Codex, Cursor and Copilot read per-agent *definition* files whose\nbody is the prompt (`4682`, `5zs1`, `uvfs`, `xxh4`).",
      "cat-harness:refs": [
        "bean 4682",
        "bean 5zs1",
        "bean uvfs",
        "bean xxh4"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "skills/conduct/conduct-core/agent-memory.md#provenance-5",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "history",
      "cat-harness:section": "The budget truncates silently, so it is checked two ways",
      "prov:value": "Checking entry *headings* against the budget is not enough, and the gap is\nworse than the one it catches. After evidence moved into detail files, one\nregion ended nine lines past the cut while its last entry's **heading** sat\ncomfortably inside — and a heading-position check returned nothing. **A\ntruncated entry is worse than a dropped one, because it still looks complete to\nthe agent reading it.** Check both: an entry whose heading falls past the\nbudget, and a region whose *end* does."
    },
    {
      "@id": "skills/conduct/conduct-core/agent-memory.md#provenance-6",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "history",
      "cat-harness:section": "Archiving is the third state, and the check order is load-bearing",
      "prov:value": "Retiring an agent **archives its memory; it does not delete it.** An archived\nentry is a node of the graph that reaches no agent's prompt — the state between\n*untagged, so reaches everybody* and *gone*. When one agent here was retired,\nseven of its twelve entries had no other reader, two of them traps written by\nother sessions, and the only remaining agent with a related subject was already\nat 189 of its 200 lines. Deleting them would have thrown away work somebody else\npaid for. **Same discipline as a scrapped work item.**"
    },
    {
      "@id": "skills/conduct/conduct-core/agent-memory.md#provenance-7",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "history",
      "cat-harness:section": "Archiving is the third state, and the check order is load-bearing",
      "prov:value": "**Archived must be checked BEFORE the untagged rule.** An archived entry has no\nagent tag once its agent is gone, so checking it second hands it to *every*\nagent — the exact opposite of archiving. Measured while doing precisely that:\ntwo entries went untagged, pushed an agent 39 lines over budget, and dropped one\nof its own traps past the line the harness truncates at."
    },
    {
      "@id": "skills/conduct/conduct-core/agent-memory.md#provenance-8",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "history",
      "cat-harness:section": "Scoping: by agent today, by role eventually",
      "prov:value": "## Scoping: by agent today, by role eventually"
    },
    {
      "@id": "skills/conduct/conduct-core/agent-memory.md#provenance-9",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "history",
      "cat-harness:section": "Scoping: by agent today, by role eventually",
      "prov:value": "Two things worth knowing before you add an entry. A CI watcher is a *mechanical*\nrole — judgement-free, which is what makes a system lane the correct home rather\nthan a convenient one — while a guard that exercises judgement is not settled by\nthe same argument, because judgement is the one thing a system lane excludes.\nAnd measured after the lanes were tagged here, **every live entry carries one**,\nso the \"untagged reaches everybody\" escape hatch currently has no instances: an\nuntagged entry added now goes to every agent in a corpus where nothing else\ndoes."
    },
    {
      "@id": "skills/conduct/conduct-core/agent-memory.md#provenance-10",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "owner-decision",
      "cat-harness:section": "The instance's two declared assets are maintained here too",
      "prov:value": "`README.md` and `AGENTS.md` are not documentation somebody else owns. They are\n**declared assets with a declared purpose**, and the owner put their upkeep in\nthis skill (2026-09-20, issue #592):\n\n> it is an asset and has a defined purpose (that is part of skills of mantiaing\n> agent memroies)",
      "cat-harness:refs": [
        "#592",
        "2026-09-20",
        "owner"
      ],
      "dcterms:date": [
        "2026-09-20"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "skills/conduct/conduct-core/agent-memory.md#provenance-11",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "owner-decision",
      "cat-harness:section": "The instance's two declared assets are maintained here too",
      "prov:value": "> it is an asset and has a defined purpose (that is part of skills of mantiaing\n> agent memroies)"
    },
    {
      "@id": "skills/conduct/conduct-core/agent-memory.md#provenance-12",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "owner-decision",
      "cat-harness:section": "The instance's two declared assets are maintained here too",
      "prov:value": "**The layer column is why a step writing one of these is a defect rather than\nan update.** The owner, on issue #592: *\"its static content at process runtime\nand treated as an asset like memories\"* — which is `context` word for word, and\nthe same layer agent memory holds, which is what *\"like memories\"* asks for.\n`processMayWriteAsset(role)` answers it, through the same `layerIsWritable`\nrule the graph typologies use: assets and directories cannot come to disagree about\nwhat `context` permits, because there is one rule and not two spellings of\n`=== \"state\"`. For a role this layer does not govern it returns `undefined` —\nthe third state, and never to be read as permission.",
      "cat-harness:refs": [
        "#592",
        "owner"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "skills/conduct/conduct-core/agent-memory.md#provenance-13",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "history",
      "cat-harness:section": "The instance's two declared assets are maintained here too",
      "prov:value": "**The delivery column is mechanical rather than a matter of taste.** Memory is\nspliced into a prompt, so it pays a budget — `MEMORY.md`'s first 200 lines,\n*with the overflow dropped silently*. A file is opened, so nothing truncates\nit. That is why `AGENTS.md` may be long and an entry here may not, and why a\nfact that must arrive unasked belongs here rather than there. It is asserted\nrather than stated: `asset-roles.test.ts` measures the longest declared\n`AGENTS.md` in the checkout — this layer's own, since the root file became a\nrendered index (2026-10-09) — past the 200-line budget, which is legal *only*\nbecause its delivery is `file` — switch the role to `injected` and the test\nsays so before a reader finds out by losing half the file.",
      "cat-harness:refs": [
        "2026-10-09"
      ],
      "dcterms:date": [
        "2026-10-09"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "skills/conduct/conduct-core/agent-memory.md#provenance-14",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "owner-decision",
      "cat-harness:section": "The instance's two declared assets are maintained here too",
      "prov:value": "> agents.md should give good coldstart instructions (dont duplicatae readme.md)\n> but augment."
    },
    {
      "@id": "skills/conduct/conduct-core/agent-memory.md#provenance-15",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "history",
      "cat-harness:section": "The instance's two declared assets are maintained here too",
      "prov:value": "`bun run cat check:subgraph-coverage` reports any instance missing either asset,\nand `bun run cat check:asset-roles` keeps the declaration single: a required role\n`ASSET_ROLES` does not govern, a role a running process would be allowed to\nwrite, or an asset restating `purpose`, `layer` or `delivery` on itself. That\nlast one is asked of the RAW declaration on purpose — `KgAssetSchema` is a\nnon-strict `z.object` and drops an unknown key without a word, so after\nparsing the evidence is gone. It has happened: a `purpose` written into\n`cat-harness/cat-harness.json` read as if it carried one and no consumer ever saw\nit, which is worse than absent because absent is visible.\nBoth are required of every instance: one with a README and no `AGENTS.md` is\nreadable by a person and mute to an agent, and the reverse leaves a reader\nfollowing instructions with nothing saying what they are in."
    }
  ]
}
